Summary: An (unwritten?) Columbia U. computer policy apparently
violates the University's free expression policy. The computer policy
has apparently been used to ban an off-site user from Columbia's
Netnews facilities.

Mr. Mealey, a BBS sys op, reports that computer officials at Columbia
U. say: "use of 4 letter words on Internet by Univ students is an
automatic cancellation of the account." He names Dr. George Rupp,
president of Columbia U. (212 854-2825) as his university contact.

An off-site Netnews poster, "Sylvia" of Hahnemann University has
apprently been banned from Columbia U.'s. Netnews facilities under
this rule. The ban followed an official complaint by Mr. Mealey about
something Sylvia wrote to soc.motss, a public forum.

What did Sylvia write? In soc.motss she wrote:
        'Seeing as how he was burned on soc.motss, most people would
        just say "Fuck it."  He, instead, saw one source of conflict
        and immediately offered to help.'
and:
        'As a conscienscious user, he is an asset to your BBS.
         Treat him with care and kindness, and stop fucking up his
         private email.'

I tried to find what written rules this might violate. Columbia U.'s
Center for Telecommunications and Research prohibits: "Frivolous,
disturbing, or otherwise inconsiderate conduct in the terminal areas,
as well as the use of terminals for sending nuisance messages,
wasteful use of the facilities for making signs, posters, calendars,
resumes, etc., on the printers."
  [ftp://ftp.eff.org/ftp/CAF/policies/ctr.columbia.edu]

The University's Academic Information systems prohibits:
     "13. Frivolous, disruptive or inconsiderate conduct in the computer
         labs or terminal areas is not permitted.

     14. No AcIS system may be used for sending nuisance messages such
         as chain letters and obscene or harassing messages."
  [ftp://ftp.eff.org/ftp/CAF/policies/columbia.edu]

Neither explicitly prohibit 4-letter words.

One would think that if 4-letter words were prohibited, they would be
prohibited by the University's "Guidelines for Civil Speech and
Conduct". But according to the Feburary 1993 draft of those
guidelines, such words are generally protected by the University's
respect for free expression. 
[gopher -p"1/publications" gopher.cc.columbia.edu 71
then select: "Civil Speech and Conduct, Guidelines for, February 1993"]

The Guidelines say:

         "It should be emphasized that these guidelines do not recommend 
         a disciplinary response for any speech that would be 
         Constitutionally protected."

The Supreme Court has ruled on the use of the word "fuck" in a student
forum. "[I]n Papish v. University of Missouri, 410 U.S.  667, 93 S.Ct.
1197, 35 L.Ed.2d 618 (1973), the Supreme Court ordered the
reinstatement of a university student expelled for distributing an
underground newspaper sporting the headline "Motherfucker acquitted"
on the grounds that "the mere dissemination of ideas--no matter how
offensive to good taste--on a state university campus may not be shut
off in the name alone of conventions of decency."  Id. at 670, 93
S.Ct. at 1199.  Although the Supreme Court acknowledged that
reasonable restrictions on the time, place, and manner of distribution
might have been permissible, "the opinions below show clearly that
[plaintiff] was dismissed because of the disapproved content of the
newspaper."  Id.  [An excerpt from _Doe v. University of Michigan_,
721 F. Supp. 852 (E.D. Mich. 1989)]

The Columbia U.'s guidelines also say:

      ==========================================================
      3. RUDENESS. We define rudeness as intentionally discourteous, 
         hostile, offensive or vulgar speech or behavior. (A pattern of 
         such rude behavior may constitute harassment, discussed below.) 
         What response, if any, may be appropriate depends on the 
         setting, the relationship of the parties and the nature of the 
         encounter.
         [...]

         b. UNOFFICIAL ENCOUNTERS. Individual or occasional instances of 
            rudeness in unofficial encounters (students jeering an 
            athlete who has made a bad play, a co-worker's use of 
            profanity in a personal conversation, for example) should 
            not, ordinarily, be the concern of the University.
        ==========================================================

Which again suggests that Sylvia's speech should have been protected
by the University, not punished by it.

- Carl
